  "name": "GLYCOSAMINOGLYCAN (GAG) MICROPOLYSACCHARIDE QUANTITATIVE",
  "code": "1110",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"154\" data-end=\"497\">The <strong data-start=\"158\" data-end=\"203\">GAG Micropolysaccharide Quantitative Test</strong> measures the amount of <strong data-start=\"227\" data-end=\"256\">glycosaminoglycans (GAGs)</strong>&mdash;a group of complex carbohydrates&mdash;in urine. This test is primarily used to <strong data-start=\"331\" data-end=\"373\">screen for mucopolysaccharidoses (MPS)</strong>, a group of <strong data-start=\"386\" data-end=\"419\">inherited metabolic disorders</strong> caused by enzyme deficiencies leading to the accumulation of GAGs in tissues.</p>\n<ul data-start=\"499\" data-end=\"912\">\n<li data-start=\"499\" data-end=\"606\">\n<p data-start=\"501\" data-end=\"606\">GAGs include <strong data-start=\"514\" data-end=\"533\">heparan sulfate</strong>, <strong data-start=\"535\" data-end=\"555\">dermatan sulfate</strong>, <strong data-start=\"557\" data-end=\"576\">keratan sulfate</strong>, and <strong data-start=\"582\" data-end=\"605\">chondroitin sulfate</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"607\" data-end=\"813\">\n<p data-start=\"609\" data-end=\"813\">MPS disorders (e.g., Hurler, Hunter, Sanfilippo syndromes) often present in <strong data-start=\"685\" data-end=\"715\">infancy or early childhood</strong> with developmental delay, skeletal abnormalities, coarse facial features, organomegaly, and more.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"814\" data-end=\"912\">\n<p data-start=\"816\" data-end=\"912\">The test is often <strong data-start=\"834\" data-end=\"863\">followed by enzyme assays</strong> or <strong data-start=\"867\" data-end=\"886\">genetic testing</strong> for definitive diagnosis.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Result</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Interpretation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Elevated GAGs</strong></td>\n<td>Suggests <strong>mucopolysaccharidosis (MPS)</strong>. The pattern of specific GAG elevation can point to the subtype.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Markedly elevated</strong></td>\n<td>May indicate a <strong>severe or untreated case</strong> of MPS.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mildly elevated</strong></td>\n<td>Could be seen in <strong>attenuated MPS forms</strong> or <strong>carriers</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
